    "uri": "https://arbor.bfh.ch/handle/arbor/32488",
    "official_url": "",
    "creators": [
      "Kilchör, Fabienne"
    ],
    "contributors": [
      "Kilchör, Fabienne"
    ],
    "abstract": [
      "Over the past few years, archaeology has experienced a rapid development in geophysical\nprospection and remote sensing techniques. At the same time, the focus of archaeological\nresearch has shifted to landscape development and human interaction. To impart the\nresults, new methods and techniques are necessary. Virtual globes such as Google Earth\noffer fascinating methods of giving interested amateurs the possibility to interactively\nexplore ancient cities and landscapes. Thanks to the increasing usage of GIS in cultural\nheritage, the implementation of interactive three dimensional learning opportunities\nbecomes less and less tedious, but the non-linear narrative story telling medium demands\nfor a special adaption of the content. This paper summarizes the experience gained during\nthe realization of the “Virtual Cilicia Project” and outlines the future potential of virtual\nglobes in the field of cultural heritage."
    ],
